INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Conti public-source investigation batch", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-002; SRC-003; SRC-004", "text": "SRC-001 Rewards for Justice states that Conti, also known as Wizard Spider, is assessed by the U.S. government as a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S. and Western critical infrastructure. It says Conti was first detected in 2019, used in more than 1,000 ransomware operations, and victimized more than 400 organizations worldwide, including more than 290 in the U.S. It describes theft and encryption, ransom portals, and publication/sale of stolen data if unpaid. It says online aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis are believed linked to Conti; associated location Russia; associated cyberthreat organization Conti/Wizard Spider; associated applications Conti ransomware-as-a-service, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, and ZLoader. SRC-002 Rapid7 reports a Feb. 27, 2022 ContiLeaks disclosure of 14MB of chats in 393 JSON files, with some encrypted and therefore incomplete; chats, screenshots, tools, exploits, design processes, credentials, alleged server access details, and alleged source code. Rapid7 assesses the leaker was likely a senior member or coalition of disgruntled affiliates. It reports hiring/interviewing, payments, salaries, product development, and a primary Bitcoin address allegedly containing over $2 billion at the time; one chat indicated possible FSB involvement in part of funding, but this is a report/assessment, not adjudicated fact. It describes Conti operating like a software company, phishing, malware development, endpoint-detection testing, control panels, infrastructure, and third-party malicious-document providers. SRC-003 CIS summarizes Conti as a notorious RaaS operation using double extortion and possible ties to TrickBot. It reports post-invasion leaks and later dismantling/rebranding into smaller units. It describes business units and roles: Coders, Testers, Administrators, Reverse Engineers, Penetration Testers/Hackers, with spamming contracted out; separate budgets; an insider claim of more than 60 staff in July 2021. It says RaaS leases malware to affiliates who conduct intrusions and share profits, often 70/30, and initial-access brokers sell access. It reports Conti efforts to acquire Cobalt Strike, pay for security/antivirus testing, and focus reverse engineering on Windows 11. SRC-004 DOJ Middle District of Tennessee identifies case numbers 3:23-CR-00092 (Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, Andrey Zhuykov) and 3:23-CR-00088 (Oleksii Lytvynenko). It states Conti affected more than 900 victims globally. DOJ says the four defendants were charged in 2023 with conspiracy to violate CFAA and conspiracy to commit wire fraud; Lytvynenko was charged in 2025 with conspiracy to deploy Conti, and the page reports a guilty plea to Count 2 in 2026. The page explicitly says defendants are presumed innocent until proven guilty beyond reasonable doubt, while reporting Lytvynenko's plea and scheduled sentencing."}
